{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And now kill every male among the little ones.</b> What business do the little ones (טַף) have here? After all, Moses' wrath did not break forth except on account of the women. And furthermore, why do I need both ״הִרְגוּ״ (a declarative/definite form) and ״הֲרֹגוּ״ (an imperative)? And it appears that Moses said to them: 'You have kept every female alive' — whether fit for intercourse or not fit — and why did you not know by reasoning that they would suspect you, that you spared the women either because you had already committed harlotry with them or because your intention is still to commit harlotry with them. And now, even if I tell you to kill the females, none the less you will not escape this suspicion, for they will say: had I not commanded you a second time, you would not have killed them. Therefore I say to you: kill every male among the little ones first and afterward the females, and then they will say that you came to take counsel with him about both of them — what the law is regarding little ones and women — for regarding the little ones there is no room to suspect you, and they will surely say that the reason you did not kill the little ones is because you were in doubt about them and came to take counsel with Moses; so too they will say regarding the women, and thereby you will escape this suspicion. And therefore he said ״הִרְגוּ״ and ״הֲרֹגוּ״, for this is not like that: for the little ones and the women each has its own reason, for the women are killed by justice, for ״הֵן הֵנָּה הָיוּ״ etc., and the little ones — in order to remove you from suspicion. And therefore they said at the end: ״וְלֹא נִפְקַד מִמֶּנּוּ אִישׁ״ — for transgression, for they said: we are clean from this suspicion." "And some say that he said ״and now״ — immediately and at once — ״kill every male among the little ones,״ for the law requires no delay and hesitation (see Berakhot 40a), but ״every woman who has known a man — kill״ — but not at once, rather only after it becomes known to you by means of the צִיץ which one is fit for intercourse. And from here is a proof for what we wrote above in parashat Tetzaveh (Exodus 28:36), that the צִיץ would atone for harlotry, as it says (Jeremiah 3:3): ״וּמֵצַח אִשָּׁה זוֹנָה הָיָה לָךְ,״ and upon it was written ״קֹדֶשׁ לַה׳,״ for in every place where there is a boundary of forbidden sexual relations, there you will find holiness. And for this reason they passed them before the צִיץ, the place of holiness, which caused that the face of every one fit for intercourse would turn pale (הוֹרִיקָה פְּנֵי). And this is a precious explanation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {0 "The phrase ״הִרְגוּ״ vs ״הֲרֹגוּ״: the former (with definite article) appears in the verse as written while the latter is the standard imperative; the Kli Yakar reads them as two distinct forms whose difference carries meaning. The final quotation of וְלֹא נִפְקַד מִמֶּנּוּ אִישׁ is from Numbers 31:49; his gloss 'for transgression' interprets לַעֲבֵרָה as the intent of the soldiers' declaration.", 1 "The Aramaic phrase ״לֵית דִּין צְרִיךְ בְּשַׁש״ (from Berakhot 40a) means literally 'this matter requires no delay'; rendered contextually. The verb הוֹרִיקָה (caused to turn pale/white) refers to the face losing color."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:43:52.611231396Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}